.TH NET 2
.SH NAME
MAC,
IP,
checksum,
put_eth,
put_arp,
parse_arp,
build_arp_request,
put_icmp_echo,
parse_icmp,
arp_insert,
arp_lookup \- ethernet, ARP and ICMP frames
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libnet"
.PP
.nf
.B "MAC :: [6]u8"
.B "IP :: [4]u8"
.B "Arp :: struct { op: u16, sha: MAC, spa: IP, tha: MAC, tpa: IP }"
.B "Icmp :: struct { kind: u8, code: u8, id: u16, seq: u16 }"
.B "Arp_Table :: struct { entries: [ARP_ENTRIES]Arp_Entry, next: int }"
.fi
.PP
.nf
.B "checksum :: proc(data: []u8, seed: u32 = 0) -> u16"
.B "put_eth :: proc(b: []u8, dst: MAC, src: MAC, ethertype: u16) -> int"
.B "put_arp :: proc(b: []u8, at: int, a: Arp) -> int"
.B "parse_arp :: proc(p: []u8) -> (a: Arp, ok: bool)"
.B "build_arp_request :: proc(b: []u8, src: MAC, spa: IP, tpa: IP) -> int"
.B "put_icmp_echo :: proc(b: []u8, at: int, kind: u8, id: u16, seq: u16, payload: []u8) -> int"
.B "parse_icmp :: proc(p: []u8) -> (m: Icmp, ok: bool)"
.B "arp_insert :: proc(t: ^Arp_Table, ip: IP, mac: MAC)"
.B "arp_lookup :: proc(t: ^Arp_Table, ip: IP) -> (MAC, bool)"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Libnet
encodes and decodes the wire formats a small IPv4 network is made of: an
ethernet frame, an ARP packet, and an ICMP message.
It touches no card and no file; it only reads and writes byte slices,
which is what lets
.B tests/net
prove it against known packets with no network present.
.PP
An
.B IP
is a four-byte IPv4 address and a
.B MAC
a six-byte ethernet address.
Every multi-byte field on the wire is big-endian, so a caller never
casts a field in or out: every procedure here does that conversion
itself.
.PP
.I Checksum
is the internet checksum of
.I data
.RI ( "RFC 1071" ),
folded onto
.IR seed .
A header carrying its own correct checksum sums to zero, which is how a
receiver checks one.
.I Seed
lets a caller carry a pseudo-header's partial sum into the sum of what
follows it.
.PP
.I Put_eth
writes a fourteen-byte ethernet header into
.I b
and answers the offset where the payload begins.
.PP
.I Put_arp
writes a 28-byte ARP packet body, ethernet over IPv4, at offset
.I at
in
.IR b ,
and answers the offset past it.
.I Parse_arp
reads one back, refusing anything that is not ethernet-over-IPv4 with
the lengths those protocols require.
.I Build_arp_request
writes a whole broadcast ARP request, ethernet header and body both,
asking who has
.IR tpa ,
and answers the frame's length.
.PP
.I Put_icmp_echo
writes an eight-byte ICMP echo or echo-reply header at
.I at
in
.IR b ,
followed by
.IR payload ,
and computes the checksum over the whole message.
.I Kind
is
.B ICMP_ECHO
or
.BR ICMP_ECHOREPLY .
.I Parse_icmp
reads one back and refuses a message whose checksum is wrong.
.PP
An
.B Arp_Table
is a small fixed set of address translations.
.I Arp_insert
records
.IR mac
for
.IR ip ,
replacing an existing entry for the same address or, when there is
none, the oldest of
.B ARP_ENTRIES
(sixteen) slots.
.I Arp_lookup
answers the hardware address recorded for
.IR ip ,
and whether one was found.
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libnet/frame.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR ether (3)
